1363856","licence":"facts only (abstract/first pages)","used_for":"owl syrinx is bronchial; one pair of intrinsic muscles; sex differences"},{"title":"Kinstler, K. A. (2009) Great Horned Owl Bubo virginianus vocalizations and associated behaviours. Ardea 97(4): 413-420","url":"https://doi.org/10.5253/078.097.0403","licence":"facts only (as summarised in search results; full text not read)","used_for":"hoots given with bill closed and throat swollen (great horned owl, not boobook)"},{"title":"Riede, T., Eliason, C. M., Miller, E. H., Goller, F. & Clarke, J. A. (2016) Coos, booms, and hoots: the evolution of closed-mouth vocal behavior in birds. Evolution 70(8): 1734-1746","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/evo.12988","licence":"facts only","used_for":"five owls (Strix varia, Bubo virginianus, Athene cunicularia, Tyto alba, Otus scops) coded open-mouth; Ninox not sampled (Table S3)"},{"title":"IOC World Bird List v15.2 (Gill, Rasmussen & Donsker, eds.)","url":"https://www.worldbirdnames.org/new/ioc-lists/master-list-2/","licence":"CC BY 4.0 - facts only","used_for":"taxonomy: order Strigiformes, family Strigidae, Ninox boobook (Latham, 1801), English name Australian Boobook (changed from Southern Boobook), 7 subspecies"},{"title":"Xeno-canto recordings listed in sources.json (own measurements)","url":"https://xeno-canto.org/species/Ninox-boobook","licence":"recordings CC BY-NC-SA; measurements are our own","used_for":"hoot note pitches, durations and spacing; braying and begging call bands"}],"science":[{"title":"Amador, Perl, Mindlin & Margoliash (2013). Elemental gesture dynamics are encoded by song premotor cortical neurons. Nature 495, 59-64","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/nature11967"},{"title":"Mindlin (2017). Nonlinear dynamics in the study of birdsong. Chaos 27, 092101","url":"https://doi.org/10.1063/1.4986932"},{"title":"Perl, Arneodo, Amador, Goller & Mindlin (2011). Reconstruction of physiological instructions from zebra finch song. Phys. Rev. E 84, 051909","ur